In article <3gjve3$4ks@tenebris.rutgers.edu>,
George Armhold <armhold@tenebris.rutgers.edu> wrote:
> I'm trying to build a BSD dump/restore suite and I've run into some
> problems with daddr_t.  In most systems this is defined to be a long
> in /usr/include/sys/types.h.  OSF/1 defines it as an int.  Perhaps I
> am misunderstanding the intended use of daddr_t, but it seems to be
> used for seeking to file offsets, so shouldn't this be a long?

daddr_t is 32 bits.

In OSF/1, a long is 64 bits.

I would have thought that OSF/1 would have gone to 64 bits for daddr_t,
but apparently not. A 32 bit daddr_t will allow volumes up to 2^32 blocks
or 2^42 bytes. By the time terabyte drives become available this will
have to be changed. Even with the LVM you'd need about 2^7 drives on a
system to exceed daddr_t today.
