In <23JAN95.11044155@tifrvax.tifr.res.in> kp@tifrvax.tifr.res.in writes:


>We have this problem on OSF1 V3.0. Hanging X session dxconsole job fills up
>the disk by writing X Toolkit Warning: Select failed; error code 9 messages
>to .xsession-errors file.

>Why and what is the solution ?

>kp@tifrvax.tifr.res.in

>xsession-errors file

>X Toolkit Warning: Cannot convert string "-*-Menu-Medium-R-Normal--*-120-*-*-P-*-ISO8859-1" to type FontStruct
>X Toolkit Warning: Cannot convert string "-*-Menu-Medium-R-Normal--*-120-*-*-P-*-ISO8859-1" to type FontStruct
>X Toolkit Warning: Select failed; error code 9

We have also experienced this problem with OSF1 V1.3 and V2.1, though not
with dxconsole.  The problem is essentially that dxconsole or any other
program that opens /dev/console and uses XtAppAddInput() with the file
descriptor for the open /dev/console, and /usr/sbin/getty on the console,
will get this warning message either when getty is started, or when
the console getty receives a HUP signal.  /usr/sbin/getty uses the revoke()
syscall before it (re)opens any terminal device, this syscall invalidates
all file descriptors that may be open for the same device, in this
case /dev/console.  Error number 9 is EBADF - Bad file number, and
the X library is getting that error when it issues a select() syscall
involving the file descriptor, unfortunately, there is no particularly
easy way for an X application to determine that this has happened
except to intercept the warning message and have the application
reopen /dev/cansole.  

In our case, the application getting the message could be modified to 
do that, but I would suspect that when using the dxconsole program, 
the only way to fix this is to adjust the starting order of getty 
and dxconsole, so that getty is started first before dxconsole tries
to open /dev/console.  Or maybe telling dxconsole to quit on an
error would work, but I wouldn't count on it, another possibility
is invoke dxconsole so that its stdout is re-directed to /dev/null,
I think that is the way that dxconsole is invoked with the DEC-supplied
xdm.


