I'm running rz/sz v1.3 (I think, it's real old in any case), since it's
what used to work on our old AT&T 3B2 under SYSVr3 (if it'll run there,
it'll run anywhere [grin]).  In any case, I've had similar problems with
ALL implementations of rz/sz that attempted to receive/transmit across a
network.  However, using the command line "rz -bevy" produced the best
results (i.e. fewest aborts/crashes, often none on smaller files).

As a caveat, I've found that Columbia's C-Kermit program rev 5A(190)
(the current one) works VERY well across the network, and at packet
sizes of 9042 with windows enabled, I get comparable throughput to
rz/sz 3.0 (when it's not running across a network). It's my protocol
of choice when I have to transport files from a non ftp-capable
machine to our Sable^H^H^H^H^HDEC 2100-A500MP, especially since it's
virtually (I've not had any) error-free in transmit & receive. You can
get a pre-compiled binary for Alpha OSF (C64, Xenix, MeSs-DOS,
Windows, etc.) from kermit.columbia.edu, as well as the C sources if
you're more comfortable compiling your own).

