I would like to have my root partition run on something other than the a
partition. I've done this before under Ultrix and it is a key component to our
cloning procedure.

However, under OSF/1 I have found that my methods don't completely work with
the new advfs.  

To do this I keep an extra kernal sitting in root directory of the a partition
(I called it /vmunix_h for running root off of the h partition).  To make this
kernel I specify the following line in the configuration file:

config          vmunix root rz2h        swap on rz2b  dumps on rz2b

So the boot procedure will load osf_boot from partition a, then it will load
vmunix_h from partition a, and then the vmunix_h kernel will jump over to h
and use it as its root.  Sounds crazy but this can be a very powerful feature
when you need to upgrade a few hundred systems.

This whole mess works when you boot off an a partition that is running ufs. It
doesn't work when the a partition is running advfs.  An error message comes
up when the boot program is loading vmunix_h.  After TEXT=<some number> is
printed an error message (something like short read) comes up and the screen
quickly clears and the system halts. One interesting point here is that the h
partition can be an advfs and it will work!

For now, I will be installing these systems running ufs on the root.

