In article <1995Jan20.091118.2578@sgcl1.unisg.ch>, g0daeniken@sgcl1.unisg.ch (Eric von Daeniken) writes:
|>What is a dnsclerk process for? Unfortunately, it's not documented in the 
|>man pages. 

The dnsclerk processes look up information in the DECdns namespaces for
DECnet and DECdns applications.

|>ps reports a 'syntax' like 
|>/usr/sbin/dnsclerk -U <username> -u <UID> -g <group> -m 0
|>
|>These processes are obviously idle, even if the user is logged out and 
|>doesn't use DECNet anymore. Can/should I kill them? (not the users, of 
|>course :-) )

No, don't kill them (if you you could corrupt the DECdns cache files).
The only supported way of getting rid of them is to disable and then
reenable the clerk.

ncl disable dns clerk
ncl enable dns clerk

I'll ask the DECdns folks to look into this ...

