Yes, there are some problems regarding dxterm and resource files.  The
best way to workaround these problems is as explained in the release
notes for the soon to be released DEC OSF/1 V3.2 version of the OS:

        Problems with the ~/DXterm Resource File

               When you use the Save Options option, dxterm saves
               its entire resource database to a file named DXterm
               in the user's home directory.  This can cause
               problems in the following situations:

               o  You use dxterm on screens with different depths.

               o  You try to override some settings in the resource
                  file with command line switches.

               o  You use the -setup switch to prevent ~/DXterm from
                  being read.


               To avoid these problems, do the following when using
               dxterm resource files:

               1. Do not use the Save Options option to create the
                  resource file.  Use it only to determine the
                  resource names and default values.

               2. Edit the resource file manually to only contain
                  the resources that are different than the system
                  default values.

               3. Do not use a ~/DXterm file as the resource file.
                  Instead, specify an alternate resource file name
                  by using the -setup switch.  Alternatively, use
                  the -xrm switch to specify any resources you want
                  to change.

I hope this helps.  If you have any questions, feel free to contact me
directly at the address below.
 
