In article <3ev64o$i7q@seagoon.newcastle.edu.au>,
tony@newcastle.edu.au (Tony Dyson) wrote:

> Can I repartition the boot disk without re-installing OSF? From the
> little I have managed to work out, it would seem not, since I would
> have to unmount all the filesystems on my machine to use disklabel
> on the boot disk, if I understand the manual correctly. I need to shrink
> the /usr partition so that I can allocate more scratch space for 
> computations.
> 
> 	Tony

If you're going to the trouble, I usually do this:

Make the d partition the same as the g+h partitions.
Go to single user.
Back up /usr and /var.
Create an AdvFS domain (called usr, say) on d.
Create filesets usr#usr and usr#var.
Restore /usr to usr#usr and /var to usr#var.
Modify /etc/fstab.
Reboot.

Now /usr and /var will share the whole d (=g+h) partition, and you'll
never have to worry about repartitioning again. In fact, if you
find the domain too small, you can move it to another disk without
even shutting the system down, if you have AdvFS Utilities.

PJDM
