Yeah, I know that, and was sort of leaning on that as I said said it
works pretty well.  On the other hand, we have a number of customers
who aren't familiar with the reputation rpc.lockd has (and so richly
deserved (note past tense)), and do squawk loudly when things go wrong.
Unfortunately, even the task of discovering that the problem is
related to file locking is a challenge, so I suspect some people never
bother to file a bug report because they don't know what to say.

>Rutgers has used lock files in many configurations for quite a long
>time, so I'm pretty confident that it works OK.

As far as I know, locking via "ln file file.lock" should work okay.  I've
thought about it a little, and I keep that in mind whenever visiting that
part of the code.  I'm not too sure about any other form.  I *encourage*
people to use rpc.lockd and report problems, at least those that are *our*
problems and not SunOS's!  I wish I had time to merge up my local tcpdump
with LBL's and get it back to them as it supports NLM and NFS V3 decoding,
and I wish we had a few spare months to work on rpc.lockd, but neither
get have the priority that V3, SMP performance, etc. have.

I'm a little reluctant to declare an "open season" on rpc.lockd, as the
the support people would probably hang me in effigy (if not in person), so
let me just reiterate that I think our rpc.lockd is one of the better ones
around.  Of course, you all may have to "upgrade" from SunOS to OSF/1 to
benefit from it, but I'm sure everyone in this building would say that's
a fine thing to do.  :-)

A couple other points:

I don't know if Sun has/will ported the Solaris rpc.lockd to SunOS.  Their
transport-independent interface offers some challeneges.  The current
OSF/1 rpc.lockd won't work on Ultrix, because the KLM protocol versions are
different.  (The new KLM protocol has 64 bit offsets and is used for both
NFS V2 and V3.)  However, we have gotten some of the new code in OSF/1's
rpc.lockd back into Ultrix, so things should be improving even there.
