> >I use it regularly with no problems.

> When you say a "real serial line" do you mean a direct serial cable 
> connection between two computers or a serial link between two computers 
> connected by phone modems?

I mean "real serial line" as in the RS-232 style port on the computer.
It doesn't matter whether it's connected to a modem or directly
to another computer, you still have to do something weird (it does
work though --- I know of at least one person using it that way).

The real distinction is with the kind of device driver used by
OSF/1.  If you're using a device with a STREAMS driver, everything
works beautifully --- this includes the pseudo-tty driver that is
used over an rlogin or telnet session.  If you're using a device
with a BSD-style driver (such as the tty drivers supplied by DEC),
then you've basically got to rlogin to your own machine to get
a STREAMS driver to push modules onto.  If you don't understand
that, don't worry about it --- the only thing you really have
to know is what kind of driver is on the line you want to use.
If you log in on the line you'd like to use for the PPP connection,
then this simple program will tell you which type of line it is:

=========
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    if (isastream(0))
        fprintf(stderr, "stdin is a STREAMS file.  PPP will work fine\n");
    else
        fprintf(stderr, "stdin is not a STREAMS file.  Need the kludge!\n");

    return(0);
}
=========

