Can someone tell me why the mountd daemon on DEC OSF systems (both 1.3 and 2.0)
don't honor the MOUNTPROC_UMNTALL call?  This is the RPC telling an
nfs server that the originating machine is done with using nfs file systems
on that server.  It is supposed to remove all entries for the caller system
from the mountd's internal usage table, as shown by showmount -a, put apparently
doesn't on DEC Alphas running the above OS versions.

Incidentally, the RPC MOUNTPROC_UMNT (unmount individual filesystems) works
fine (thank god).

The UMNTALL RPC is used by the 'umount -a -t nfs' command that is part
of many systems bootup sequence, as well as by automounters.
The consequence of this bug is that, depending on client software and usage
patterns, stale entries accumulate in an OSF server's mountd. The most
glaring bad side effect is that when such a server is shut down, it 
sends its warning messages all over the network, even to machines that 
don't use any of its file systems.

I hope DEC people read this, since I don't have administrative control
over any DEC OSF systems themselves, we just have to interoperate with them.

