Hello everybody,

we stumbeled over this piece of fortran-code yesterday

      program another_bug

      i=256
      print *, (2*i-1)*4194304
                     
      end

When compiled with f77 and default options, this works as expected. 
BUT, when I use f77 -check overflow it aborts:

>f77 -check overflow t.f
>a.out
forrtl: error: floating point exception
IOT trap (core dumped)
>

According to the manpage -check overflow generates traps on interger
overflows. 

Now, this behavior is STRANGE (at least to me) because

first, the above program only does INTEGER calculations but the system
reports a FLOATING point exception.

second, the above expression DOES NOT RESULT in an overflow. The expession

	(2*256 - 1)*4194304			(a)

can be written as

	(2**8 - 1) * 2**22			(b)

which is LESS than 2**31. Maybe the compiler evaluates it as

        2**8 * 2**22 - 2**22			(c)

That would explain trap (but not the floating exception errormessage).
My question ist: Is the compiler free to do such transformations as
from (a) to (c), which are mathematically (but not numerically)
equivalent? If so, is there a way to stop it? Or at least a way of
knowing which transformations where applied (i.e. a way to find out
whats going on :-) ).

BTW, this discussion is based on the assumption of 32bit integers, we
are running on OSF1 V3.0 347 alpha and DEC Fortran V3.5-576.

Any ideas?

Thanks

joerg

  



