You want to change the behavior of the shell.  Under OSF/1, DEC has
provided a smarter version of csh.  This can be activated by putting
the line:
set editmode=emacs  # or =vi
In a user's .cshrc file. (assuming the user is using csh). Look at the
cmdedit(1) man page for details.

I think ksh will do this too, but I don't use ksh, so I don't know how
it works.

A solution that will work on almost any unix is to start using a
public domain shell such as tcsh, bash or zsh.  These shells have
command line editing, filename completion, and more.

Tcsh is built on top of csh, doing most of what DEC has done for csh &
a whole lot more.  Its available via anon ftp from
tesla.ee.cornell.edu, in the directory /pub/tcsh

Bash is built on top of sh, and has similar functionality to DEC's
csh, or tcsh, but sh-like syntax.  Its available from any gnu mirror
site. 

I've never used zsh, but people say its wonderful... 

Lets not start any shell wars. ;^}

Drew

