>We are looking at adding a new hard drive to our DEC Alpha 3000 model
>400 and would like know what we are getting into. First, what kind of
>drive do we have to have? Does it need to be SCSI-2, or can it be just
>SCSI?

SCSI-2 will be faster.  Most SCSI-1 devices will work.

> If SCSI-2, is it Fast, Wide, or neither?

Not fast/wide.  Just SCSI-2.

> Also, what vendors would
>be likely to have disktab entries for, say, a 4 GB drive?

You don't need a disktab entry.  OSF/1 disktab will probe for the
parameters if you just specify a disk name that doesn't appear in 
/etc/disktab (eg, call it "foo")

> We've looked
>at some Quantum and Seagate drives, but haven't seen anyone mention the
>fact that they support the OSF/1 unix (or any unix systems for that
>matter).

Most SCSI-2 drives will work with the Alphas.  We use a mixture of
Conner, Quantum, Micropolis, Toshiba and DEC drives on our Alphas.
The 2.1Gb 3.5" Seagate Barracudda did not work for us.  That was
a notable exception (it just hung the bus in every configuration,
but worked perfectly in a Sun).

> We would like to deal with someone that has some experience
>with this sort of thing, since it is new for us. We know that the
>obvious choice is Digital, but they seem to be about 60-100% higher
